          Most of the names on water heaters in North America trace back to a few parent companies, and within each family the tanks are close cousins. The brand on the label matters less than the warranty printed beside it, the model tier you choose, and whether a local installer can get parts for it on a Saturday. This guide maps the families, then shows how to choose between them.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: A Few Companies Make Most of Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you shop for a tank water heater in the United States, you are almost certainly choosing between products from three manufacturers: Rheem, A.O. Smith and Bradford White. Each sells under more than one name, and the extra names exist mostly to give different retailers, distributors and contractors a line of their own. When two labels come from the same parent, the tank, the anode, the gas valve or the heating elements are often shared or nearly so.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tankless is a slightly wider field. Rinnai, Navien and Noritz built their reputations on tankless gas units, Rheem and A.O. Smith both sell tankless lines alongside their tanks, and Takagi has a long history in the category. Electric tankless adds names like Stiebel Eltron and EcoSmart. Even so, the list of companies that genuinely design and build heat exchangers is short, and most of what you see online is one of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            That concentration is good news for buyers. It means there are very few truly bad water heaters from the major families, because the same engineering, the same certification testing and the same safety standards sit behind every label. A water heater is a steel tank or a copper or stainless heat exchanger wrapped around a burner or an element, and the main makers have been refining that recipe for decades. The difference between brands is narrower than the price gap suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where the brands do differ is in the things that decide what owning the heater is like: how long the warranty runs, what it actually covers, whether a plumber near you stocks parts, and which model tiers each retailer carries. That is why the rest of this guide spends more time on warranties and service than on logos. If you are still deciding between tank, tankless and heat pump, read <a href="/types-of-water-heaters/">our guide to the types of water heaters</a> first, because the type matters far more than the brand.
          </p>

