        <div id="tank-makers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes Tank Water Heaters
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Rheem family: Rheem, Ruud and Richmond</h3>

          <p>
            Rheem has been owned by the Japanese company Paloma since the late 1980s and is one of the largest water heater makers in North America. It sells under three main names. Rheem is the retail and consumer face, and its Performance and Performance Platinum lines are the tank heaters you see stacked at Home Depot. Ruud is the sister brand sold through plumbing and HVAC distributors to contractors. Richmond is the name Rheem uses for the line sold at Menards.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical result is that a Rheem, a Ruud and a Richmond of similar capacity and fuel type are built on closely related platforms. Model numbers, jacket colors and warranty terms differ, and the contractor line sometimes carries features or warranty options the store line does not, but you are not buying three different engineering philosophies. Rheem also makes tankless gas units, electric tankless units and the ProTerra heat pump water heater, so one family covers every type of heater most homes would consider.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The A.O. Smith family: A.O. Smith, State, American and Lochinvar</h3>

          <p>
            A.O. Smith is a Milwaukee-based, publicly traded company and the other giant of the tank market. Over the years it has bought several well-known names. State Water Heaters joined in 2001, American Water Heater came with a larger acquisition in 2006, and Lochinvar, best known for boilers and high-efficiency commercial heaters, followed in 2011. Each brand still has its own distributor relationships and product lines, but they share a corporate parent, engineering resources and much of the supply chain.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a homeowner, A.O. Smith is the name you will see at Lowe's, State and American are mainly contractor and distributor brands, and Lochinvar tends to show up on higher-efficiency and larger equipment. A.O. Smith also sells tankless units and the Voltex heat pump water heater. As with Rheem, the label you pick inside the family matters less than the model tier, the anode and the warranty that comes with it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bradford White: the independent sold only through pros</h3>

          <p>
            Bradford White is the odd one out. It is a privately held American company, and it deliberately does not sell through big-box stores or online retailers; its heaters reach homes through licensed plumbing and heating contractors. That policy is why many plumbers default to it. They can buy it from their supply house, the company supports them rather than competing with them at retail, and parts are stocked through the same channel. Bradford White makes gas and electric tanks, the AeroTherm heat pump water heater and a tankless line. You cannot walk out of a store with one, which is the point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tankless-makers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Tankless Water Heater Brands
          </h2>

          <p>
            Japanese manufacturers were early leaders in tankless gas heaters, and several of the biggest names in the category are still Japanese. Rinnai, headquartered in Japan, is one of the most widely installed tankless brands in North America and has a deep network of trained installers and parts distributors. Noritz is another Japanese manufacturer with a long tankless history and a strong presence among contractors. Takagi is also a Japanese tankless specialist; its units are sold widely through distributors and online. If you want to understand how these units work before comparing badges, <a href="/what-is-a-tankless-water-heater/">our explainer on what a tankless water heater is</a> covers the burner, the flow sensor and the heat exchanger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Navien, part of the South Korean KD Navien group, built its North American business on condensing tankless heaters. Its best-known feature is a model line with a small built-in buffer tank and recirculation pump, which reduces the wait for hot water and softens the cold-water sandwich that tankless owners complain about. Like other condensing tankless units, Navien's models can typically vent with plastic pipe rather than stainless steel, which often lowers the installed cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rheem and A.O. Smith round out the gas tankless field with their own lines, which gives buyers the option of matching brands if they already know a local Rheem or A.O. Smith dealer. Their tankless units compete directly with Rinnai and Navien on flow and efficiency, and they are easier to find at retail. Our <a href="/best-tankless-water-heater/">tested tankless water heater picks</a> rank specific models across these brands by real winter flow rather than the rating on the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric tankless is a separate market with different leaders. Stiebel Eltron, a German company, has a strong reputation for well-built whole-house and point-of-use electric units. EcoSmart and Rheem sell large numbers of electric tankless heaters at retail,; other makers sell compact units aimed at single sinks. Because an electric tankless lives or dies on your panel capacity and groundwater temperature, the brand is the last decision, not the first. Our <a href="/best-electric-tankless-water-heater/">electric tankless guide</a> and <a href="/point-of-use-water-heater/">point-of-use water heater guide</a> cover sizing before brand.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Pick the type first, then the brand.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A well-sized heater from any major family will outperform a badly sized one from the brand you trust most. Settle tank versus tankless versus heat pump, then capacity, then compare the two or three brands your installer actually supports.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="retail-vs-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Store Brands vs Pro Brands: Is There a Real Difference?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common question about water heater brands is whether the heater from the home center is worse than the one a plumber installs. The honest answer is that it depends more on the model tier than on the channel. The big retailers each lean on one family: Home Depot sells Rheem, Lowe's carries A.O. Smith, and Menards sells Rheem-built Richmond heaters. Inside those lines there are budget, mid-range and premium models, and the budget models are budget for real reasons.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cheapest tank in any line usually has a shorter warranty, a thinner anode rod or a single anode instead of two, less insulation and a more basic gas valve or element setup. Step up a tier and you usually get a longer tank warranty, better anode protection and sometimes a self-cleaning dip tube or improved burner. Contractor lines like Ruud, State and Bradford White are organized the same way. Comparing a premium store model with a base contractor model can easily favor the store unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the pro channel does tend to offer is service. A plumber who installs Bradford White or Ruud every week knows the products, has warranty contacts at the supply house and can often get a replacement part or even a replacement heater the same day. A store-bought heater is just as likely to be covered by the manufacturer, but you may be the one making the warranty call, and some retail warranties require proof of professional installation or registration within a set window.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price is the other variable. A store heater lets you separate the cost of the unit from the cost of labor, while a contractor quote bundles them. The bundled quote usually includes a markup on the heater, but it also includes haul-away, code updates like an expansion tank or seismic straps, and someone accountable if the install leaks. If you are replacing a tank with a tankless, the labor side dominates the bill; our <a href="/replace-water-heater-with-tankless/">guide to replacing a water heater with tankless</a> breaks down what that work involves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranties" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Warranties Are the Real Difference Between Brands
          </h2>

          <p>
            Since the major families build comparable hardware, the warranty is the clearest signal of what the manufacturer expects from a given model. For tank heaters, residential tank warranties commonly fall somewhere between six and twelve years, with the longest terms reserved for premium models. The same company will sell a six-year and a twelve-year version of a similar-looking heater, and the difference is rarely cosmetic. Longer warranties usually come with more anode protection and better components, because the maker is the one paying if the tank leaks early.
          </p>

          <p>
            For tankless heaters, look at three numbers separately: the heat exchanger warranty, the parts warranty and the labor warranty. Residential heat exchanger coverage from the major tankless brands often runs from ten to fifteen years, parts coverage is typically around five years, and labor coverage is often only one year or absent entirely. A long heat exchanger term is good, but the board, fan or flow sensor that fails in year six may be the part you actually pay for. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-tankless-water-heaters-last/">how long tankless water heaters last</a> compares what those terms mean over the life of the unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read the conditions as carefully as the headline years. Many warranties are reduced or voided if the heater is not installed by a licensed professional, if it is installed in a commercial setting, if the unit is not registered within a set period, or if damage is caused by scale from untreated hard water. Tankless warranties in particular often require that the unit is descaled on schedule in hard-water areas, which is a good reason to keep receipts for maintenance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, remember what a warranty does not cover. A tank warranty usually replaces the heater, not the labor to install the replacement, the water damage from a leak, or the permit fees. That is why a slightly cheaper heater with a strong labor warranty from your installer can be worth more than a premium unit installed by someone who will not answer the phone in year three. Specific terms change from model to model and year to year, so always check the exact warranty sheet for the model number you are quoted.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Warranty Ranges</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">6–12 yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Residential tank warranty</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">10–15 yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tankless heat exchanger</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~5 yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tankless parts</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0–1 yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Manufacturer labor</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="service" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Parts, Installers and Service Matter More Than the Logo
          </h2>

          <p>
            A water heater that fails tends to fail at an inconvenient time, and how fast it comes back depends on parts availability. Tank heaters are simple and parts like thermocouples, elements, thermostats and anode rods are widely stocked. Gas control valves and the sealed combustion components on power-vent and high-efficiency models are more brand-specific, and that is where a local distributor for your brand starts to matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tankless heaters are more complex, with circuit boards, fans, flow sensors, igniters and sometimes recirculation pumps. A brand with a strong local distributor and trained installers will usually get you running faster than a brand you bought online with no one nearby who services it. Before you choose, it is worth calling two plumbers and asking which tankless brands they install and stock parts for. If they all name the same one or two, that is useful information.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installer familiarity also affects the quality of the installation itself. Venting rules, gas line sizing, condensate drains and recirculation setups differ between brands and models. A contractor who installs one brand regularly is less likely to make a mistake that shortens the heater's life or voids the warranty. Many of the problems people blame on a brand come from installation errors such as undersized gas lines, poor venting or missing isolation valves for descaling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tank heaters fail in more predictable ways, mostly from corrosion once the anode is spent, and the brand does less to change that than maintenance does. Flushing sediment and replacing the anode at the right time can add years to almost any tank. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/">how long water heaters last</a> covers the warning signs and how to read the serial number to find a heater's age.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-pick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Pick a Brand for Your House
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the type of heater. For a standard gas tank, any of the three major families is a sound choice, and our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">gas water heater rankings</a> compare specific models by first-hour rating and venting. For a heat pump water heater, Rheem, A.O. Smith and Bradford White all sell credible units, and our <a href="/best-heat-pump-water-heater/">heat pump water heater guide</a> covers which suits your space. For gas tankless, the shortlist is Rinnai, Navien, Noritz, Rheem and A.O. Smith.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, decide whether you are buying through a store or a contractor. If you are buying at retail, your brand choice is largely made by the store you shop at, so focus on choosing the right tier within that line: a longer warranty and better anode protection are usually worth a modest premium. If you are hiring a plumber, ask which brands they install and service, and lean toward the one they know best. Their familiarity is worth more than a marginal difference between labels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then compare warranties line by line. Put the tank or heat exchanger term, the parts term and the labor term side by side, and ask the installer what labor warranty they add. Ask what the warranty requires from you, such as registration, maintenance records or water treatment, and whether your water hardness makes descaling necessary. A brand that looks cheaper but needs more maintenance in your water may not be cheaper over ten years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, size the heater correctly. The wrong size causes more complaints than the wrong brand, from cold showers with an undersized tank to tankless units that cannot keep up in winter. Settle the capacity and fuel first, choose the brand your installer supports, and pick the model tier with the warranty you want. That sequence produces a better result than shopping for the most recognizable name.
          </p>

