        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the brand before the type</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The decision between tank, tankless and heat pump affects comfort and running cost far more than the logo. Picking a favorite brand first can lock you into a heater type that does not suit your fuel, your panel or your hot water demand, and no amount of brand quality fixes that mismatch later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing a budget model with a premium one</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every family sells several tiers. Comparing a base model from one brand with a top model from another tells you about the tiers, not the brands. Compare models with similar warranties, anode protection and efficiency ratings, or you will draw the wrong conclusion about which company builds better heaters.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the warranty conditions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Headline warranty years mean little if the conditions are not met. Many require professional installation, registration within a set window, or descaling in hard water. Read the requirements before buying, register the heater promptly, and keep receipts for maintenance so a claim is not rejected when you need it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a brand nobody local services</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A tankless heater from a brand no nearby plumber supports can leave you waiting days for a part. Before buying online, check that a local contractor or distributor stocks parts for that brand. Service availability usually matters more than small differences in specifications or price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming store heaters are worse</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A store heater is not automatically inferior. Retail and contractor lines from the same family share engineering, and a premium store model can outlast a base contractor model. Judge the model on its warranty, anode protection and installation quality rather than on where it was sold.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best water heater brand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no single best brand. Rheem, A.O. Smith and Bradford White all make reliable tank heaters, and Rinnai, Navien and Noritz are well regarded for tankless. The best choice is usually the brand your installer knows and services, in a model tier with the warranty length you want, sized correctly for your household.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes Richmond water heaters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Richmond is a Rheem brand. It is the line sold at Menards, and it shares engineering with Rheem's other tank heaters. Model numbers and warranty terms can differ from the Rheem-branded heaters sold elsewhere, so compare the specific warranty sheet, but you are buying a heater from the same manufacturer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Rheem and Ruud the same company?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Ruud is a Rheem brand sold mainly through plumbing and HVAC distributors to contractors, while the Rheem name is used for retail and consumer channels. Comparable models are built on closely related platforms, though the contractor line may offer different features or warranty options than the retail line.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why can I not buy Bradford White at a store?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bradford White sells only through licensed plumbing and heating contractors by design. The company does not supply big-box or online retailers, which is why many plumbers favor it: they are not competing with retail pricing and their supply house stocks the parts. To get one, you need a contractor to supply and install it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which tankless water heater brand is most reliable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rinnai, Navien, Noritz, Rheem and A.O. Smith all sell tankless units with long heat exchanger warranties, and none is clearly unreliable. Reliability in practice depends heavily on correct sizing, gas line and venting work, and regular descaling in hard water. Choose a brand with local parts support and an installer experienced with it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do store-bought water heaters have worse warranties?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not necessarily. Retail lines are sold in several warranty tiers, and premium store models often carry long tank warranties. Some retail warranties do require professional installation or registration, and manufacturer warranties rarely cover labor. Compare the exact terms for the model number, and ask any installer what labor warranty they add.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the brand affect how long a water heater lasts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Less than most people think. Within the major families, lifespan depends more on the model tier, anode protection, water quality and maintenance than on the label. Flushing sediment, replacing the anode on time and descaling tankless units in hard water usually do more for lifespan than switching brands.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

